# Component-First

This prototype represents the frontend of a CMS. Every block of every page is a component.

## Order of work

1. Define the component in `assets/js/components.js` as a function returning an HTML string.
2. Register it in `COMPONENT_REGISTRY` (`assets/js/site-registry.js`) so it appears on `/components`.
3. Only then assemble the page from those components.

Never write one-off markup directly into a page for a block that renders content. Page files
should read as a list of component calls plus their data.

```js
// components.js
Components.heroBanner = ({ eyebrow, title, body, actions = [] }) => `
  <section class="cmp cmp-hero">...</section>
`;
```

## Registries are the source of truth

- `PAGE_REGISTRY` drives `/sitemap`. Adding a page means adding an entry with its `path`,
  `title`, `parent`, `branch`, and `status` (`built` or `planned`), plus any cross-links.
- `COMPONENT_REGISTRY` drives `/components`. Adding a component means adding an entry with its
  `id`, `name`, `group`, `usedOn`, and sample props.

Both registry updates are part of the change — a page or component that is not registered is
not finished.

## Page connections

When a page is marked `built`, wire it into the site and document links in `PAGE_CONNECTIONS.md`.
See the `page-connections` rule. Ask the user what to connect when the target is unclear.

**After building a page, run a separate connection-audit Task** (see `page-connections` skill) before
setting `status: 'built'`. Update component `usedOn` arrays in the same pass.

## Tab URLs

Any page with tabs that change content must give each tab a shareable URL. Use `filterTabs` with
`syncUrl: true`, `urlBase`, and matching nav helpers. See the `tab-urls` rule.

## Hidden routes

`/sitemap` and `/components` are internal tools. Never link them from the site header, footer,
or any public navigation.
